---
functor: 1
title: Diagrams and labelled arrows
---

set heading(numbering: none)

A diagram is a grid with arrows. A matrix is a grid with a fence. Both use the same cells: a comma ends a cell and a semicolon ends a row.

| Structure | Positions | Relations |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `mat`, `vec`, `cases`, `binom` | a grid | a fence |
| a chain in display math | rows | the align point |
| `diagram` | a grid | arrows between cells |

# Labelled arrows

A script on a relation is a label. `^` puts the label above, and `_` puts it below. The arrow grows with `wide` to fit the label.

| Write | Result |
|:--|:-:|
| `A to^f B` | $A to^f B$ |
| `A to_p B` | $A to_p B$ |
| `x =^"def" y` | $x =^"def" y$ |
| `0 to A to^f B to^g C to 0` | $0 to A to^f B to^g C to 0$ |

This works on any relation. A relation with a long straight part, such as `to`, `=`, `implies` or `mapsto`, grows. A relation with no straight part, such as `sim`, keeps its size and stays in the middle.

# diagram

Write the cells, then `|`, then the arrows. Write each arrow as a map: `f: A to B`.

```fn
$$
diagram(
  A, B;
  C, D
| f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D)
$$
```

It gives:

$$
diagram(A, B; C, D | f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D)
$$

- An arrow names its cells by their content. If two cells have the same content, write the position: `2.1` is row 2, column 1.
- The arrow is any relation: `to`, `mapsto`, `epi`, `implies`, `=`, `cong`, `sim`, or a `sym` that you build. On a diagonal or a vertical edge, the same glyph turns to the angle.
- `mono`, `epi` and `incl` are arrow kinds. A rule such as `set mono(style: hook)` changes every mono arrow in the file. See [Meaning and style](symbols/kinds.html).
- `f: A to B` puts the label f on the outer side of the diagram. `A to^f B` puts it on the left of the arrow direction, and `A to_f B` on the right.
- A cell can be empty: `diagram(A, ; B, C | A to C)`.
- Do not write a bar `|` in a cell. Use `abs(x)`.

```fn
$$
diagram(X times Y, Y; X, Z | p_2: X times Y to Y, p_1: X times Y to X, g: Y to Z, f: X to Z, X times Y to Z)
$$
```

$$
diagram(X times Y, Y; X, Z | p_2: X times Y to Y, p_1: X times Y to X, g: Y to Z, f: X to Z, X times Y to Z)
$$

| Rule | Values | Default |
|:--|:--|:--|
| `set diagram(gap: ...)` | `small`, `normal`, `large` | `normal` |
| `set diagram(inline: ...)` | `small`, `full` | `small` |
| `set diagram(cross: ...)` | `gap`, `plain` | `gap` |
| `set diagram(bend: ...)` | `small` (15°), `normal` (30°), `large` (45°), or degrees from 1 to 80 | `normal` |

The gap between two cells also grows to fit a long label.

# Curved arrows

`bend` on the arrow makes a curved arrow. It is the same `bend` operation as on a symbol. `to.bend` curves to the left of the arrow direction, and `to.bend.flipv` curves to the right. A label goes on the outer side of the curve.

Each `bend` adds one step. One step is 30° at each end, so `to.bend.bend` curves 60°. The most is 80°. The angle is style, so one rule sets it for the file: `set diagram(bend: small)` makes one step 15°, and `set diagram(bend: 20)` makes it 20°. The gap between the cells grows with the angle.

```fn
$$
diagram(A, B; C, D | f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D, A to.bend D)
$$
```

$$
diagram(A, B; C, D | f: A to B, g: A to C, h: B to D, k: C to D, A to.bend D)
$$

# Loops

A loop is an arrow from a cell to the same cell: `e: A to A`. The loop goes to a free side of the cell. A side is free when no arrow leaves the cell in that direction and no cell is next to it there. When two sides are equally free, the loop goes to the outer side of the diagram, and above a cell that is alone.

```fn
$$
diagram(A, B | e: A to A, f: A to B)
$$
```

$$
diagram(A, B | e: A to A, f: A to B)
$$

A second loop on the same cell goes to the next free side:

```fn
$$
diagram(T | mu: T to T, eta: T to T)
$$
```

$$
diagram(T | mu: T to T, eta: T to T)
$$

`rot` and `turn` move a loop. They have the same meaning as on a symbol: `rot` turns a quarter circle to the left, and `turn` turns a half circle. `X to.rot X` puts the loop to the left of the free side, and `X to.turn X` puts it on the opposite side.

$$
diagram(X | X to X) quad diagram(X | X to.rot X) quad diagram(X | X to.turn X)
$$

A loop does not bend. On an arrow that is not a loop, `rot` and `turn` give a warning, because the cells set the direction of the arrow.

# 2-cells

A 2-cell is an arrow between two arrows. Name the two arrows with a label, then write the 2-cell with the two names. Write the two arrows first.

```fn
$$
diagram(A, B | f: A to.bend B, g: A to.bend.flipv B, alpha: f implies g)
$$
```

$$
diagram(A, B | f: A to.bend B, g: A to.bend.flipv B, alpha: f implies g)
$$

The name of an arrow is its label: `f: A to B`, `A to^f B` or `A to_f B`.

# Crossing arrows

When two arrows cross, the arrow written first gets a small gap, so the arrow written later goes over it. Write the front arrows last.

```fn
$$
diagram(A, , B, ; , A', , B'; C, , D, ; , C', , D' | A to B, A to C, B to D, C to D, A' to B', A' to C', B' to D', C' to D', A to A', B to B', C to C', D to D')
$$
```

$$
diagram(A, , B, ; , A', , B'; C, , D, ; , C', , D' | A to B, A to C, B to D, C to D, A' to B', A' to C', B' to D', C' to D', A to A', B to B', C to C', D to D')
$$

`set diagram(cross: plain)` draws crossing arrows with no gap.

# Inline structures

Inline is a context, so it is a style rule and not new syntax. In running text, `mat`, `vec` (with components in a column) and `diagram` are small by default, so the lines stay evenly spaced. `set mat(inline: full)` keeps them full size.

The matrix $mat(a, b; c, d)$ is small in text. A one-row sequence such as $0 to A to^f B to 0$ needs no diagram.

# Not in Functor 1

A bend angle for one arrow only. `set diagram(bend: ...)` sets the step for the whole file, and `bend.bend` doubles it on one arrow.
